    client: fog runs SNAP to target position, never glide
    
    Scope: client/player_client.html only. No sim, no hash, no goldens.
    
    Maxwell's report ("still getting the negative space lines in the
    shadows" -- light lines where shadow is missing) pointed at a real
    mechanism, confirmed by direct instrumentation before this fix: fog-of-war
    ships as many independent per-row "run" rectangle objects
    (src/ctf/global.nim addFogRuns), rebuilt every tick from the live vision
    cone. Every object the client tracks -- fog runs included -- was routed
    through the same motion-interpolation path used for player/bot sprites:
    Math.round(fromX+(x-fromX)*t) over ~41.7ms. Two runs that are exactly
    pixel-adjacent in the authoritative server data can round to different
    integer positions mid-glide, opening a gap that exposes the lit map layer
    through the ~37%-opacity fog (FogAlpha=160) -- a light seam in the shadow,
    not a dark band, and not a GPU/compositor phenomenon (fog pixels are
    written via exact-integer putImageData, no antialiasing involved).
    
    Confirmed on evidence, not just plausibility: instrumented dispX/dispY
    directly (no screenshots -- the ~41.7ms glide window is shorter than any
    screenshot cadence) during a 30s live aim sweep (zero WASD, camera
    provably static). 1506 of 2351 sampled interpolation ticks (64%) showed
    at least one gap between two fog runs whose target rects were exactly
    touching, magnitudes from 1px up to a full FovCellSize (8px) and
    occasionally up to a full run-width when a reused run-object index
    briefly pointed at an unrelated region.
    
    Fix: fog runs (FogLayerId=4) are excluded from the glide test and always
    take the existing snap branch (same code path already used for a layer
    change or a jump past SNAP_DISTANCE) -- they now render at their target
    position immediately, every time the server sends one. Semantically this
    is not a moving object: it is a recomputed mask, and lerping between two
    different ticks' masks was never a meaningful "motion" to smooth.
    
    Verified after rebuilding: the same instrumentation shows 0 of 1787
    sampled ticks with a gap event under identical aim-sweep load. Feel
    check (screenshot comparison, gross appearance only): fog boundaries look
    clean and normal in both the before and after builds; no visible judder
    introduced by snapping instead of easing -- unsurprising given the
    underlying vision-cone recompute is already capped at the sim's ~24Hz
    tick rate, so the removed 41.7ms ease was a very small window to begin
    with. Judgment: this minimal fix is sufficient; the fallback design
    (glide the whole fog set with one shared rounded offset) is not needed.
    
    Built from deployed/swap7-20260830 (c6c27ca7fc269e475a43ad32a9c1f540d29349c4),
    the exact commit confirmed byte-identical (MD5 e1f0ca3ce2b00fd4e1d56456099446d6)
    to what live prod (100.102.207.18:7420) serves.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

